<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prudentius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">348–after 405</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Aurelius Prudentius Clemens</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prudentius (Aurelius Prudentius Clemens) was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Christian poet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, probably born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calagurris">Calagurris</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calahorra">Calahorra</a>). After a career as a provincial governor and official at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>, he turned to religious verse, writing the Cathemerinon, the Peristephanon on the martyrs, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eulalia-of-merida">Eulalia of Mérida</a>, and the Psychomachia, an allegory of the soul's struggle. He was one of the most influential poets of late antiquity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prudentius.jpg" alt="Prudentius" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BLAdd24199PrudentiusFol11v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
